Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.680
Groupon, one of the fastest growing companies in&nbsp;
history, a 12 billion IPO, 35 million subscribers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:14.000
and an offer from Google for $6&nbsp;
billion, which they turned down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:22.240
Today, Groupon is worth less than 300 million.&nbsp;
The founder was fired with severance of $378. Yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:28.200
that's not even the strangest part. accounting&nbsp;
fraud, outrageous and false advertising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:34.480
a giant Ponzi scheme, misleading investors,&nbsp;
all to give founders one big payday.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:39.600
This is the story of Groupon, one of&nbsp;
the strangest tech companies in history.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:51.040
Okay, so what the heck is this company?&nbsp;
Think about the last time you tried to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:56.800
cancel a subscription. The hidden fees,&nbsp;
the endless steps, the annoying support.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:03.960
The company has all the power and you have none.&nbsp;
What if you could change that? Andrew Mason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:09.600
and Eric Leovsky felt this very experience&nbsp;
trying to cancel an annoying phone plan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:16.600
And then they had an idea. In business, there's&nbsp;
a concept called bargaining power of buyers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:22.320
If you're one customer of many, you have no&nbsp;
leverage. But if you're one of few customers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:27.360
or you get thousands of customers together,&nbsp;
suddenly the price moves in your direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:36.720
So why not just do that? Mason turned that concept&nbsp;
into Groupon, a marketplace plus a coupon engine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:42.040
Get a massive group of buyers together and use&nbsp;
that collective power to get better discounts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:48.760
and better terms. A local merchant, like a&nbsp;
restaurant, offers a steep discount, say 50%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:54.240
Customers buy a voucher through Groupon instead.&nbsp;
Groupon collects the money, keeps its cut, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:00.000
passes some to the merchant. The merchant gets new&nbsp;
customers, the customer gets a deal, and Groupon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.800
gets a cut. Sounds great. Unfortunately, this&nbsp;
model had a gigantic gaping flaw. But for now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:14.800
Groupon was spreading and fast. Groupon launched&nbsp;
in November 2008. By the same time in 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:21.280
Groupon had over 35 million [snorts] subscribers.&nbsp;
Over six million were on the daily mailing list.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:26.920
Forb said the company is on pace to make 1 billion&nbsp;
in sales faster than any other business ever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:34.040
2,700 employees, coupon discounts in 300&nbsp;
cities valued at 1 billion in just 16 months.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:41.160
They were attracting a lot of attention&nbsp;
[music] and not just from users. In late 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:47.200
Google offered to buy Groupon and [music] you can&nbsp;
see why. They wanted the algorithm, the merchant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:53.440
network, the 35 million email subscribers.&nbsp;
Great for Google. Google started negotiations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:59.680
at $3 billion. Over several [music] weeks of back&nbsp;
and forth, the number climbed to 5.75 billion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:05.120
the largest acquisition offer in internet history&nbsp;
at the time, just ahead of Yahoo's acquisition of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:11.960
Broadcast.com at 5.7 billion. Every board member&nbsp;
would gain hundreds of millions. Co-founder Eric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:19.600
was looking at 1.8 8 billion and Mason would have&nbsp;
pocketed 420 million. But there was a problem. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:25.720
deal might not even go through. Google was already&nbsp;
facing two separate antitrust investigations at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:31.880
the time. The board feared the deal could be tied&nbsp;
up in antitrust review potentially for 18 months.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:39.040
The Justice Department could ultimately kill it&nbsp;
altogether. But Google really wanted Groupon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:44.320
So to mitigate the risk, they offered an $800&nbsp;
million breakup fee if [music] that happened.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:50.320
But even with that, if the deal collapsed after&nbsp;
a year in limbo, [music] Groupon could be ruined.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:54.960
Growth would stall, hiring [music] would&nbsp;
freeze, competitors would catch up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.960 --> 00:04:00.840
They had to make a decision. On December 3rd,&nbsp;
they called Google and [music] killed the deal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:05.360
Mason later said, "We went through a&nbsp;
period of introspection and self-doubt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:10.680
and then ultimately emerged in a state&nbsp;
of supreme confidence, like, okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:15.320
we're the best company in the world." Groupon&nbsp;
believed they could be worth 10 times more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:22.960
what Google offered. Groupon was ready to go, but&nbsp;
some of their confidence may have been misplaced.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:36.480
After rejecting Google in January 2011, Groupon&nbsp;
raised $950 million in a round of funding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:41.240
the largest round of its kind for an&nbsp;
internet [snorts] company at the time. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:47.560
this was nothing. What they were really gearing&nbsp;
up for was an IPO which could be worth billions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:52.000
That was the real payday. New York&nbsp;
investment bankers projected Groupon's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:57.880
valuation at anywhere from 20 billion to 30&nbsp;
billion, far more than what Google offered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:03.160
Groupon's valuation soared to such heights in&nbsp;
just 3 years of existence. Bloomberg News is now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:09.480
reporting that Groupon may even increase the price&nbsp;
range in another. But there were two problems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:16.560
One, Groupon wasn't profitable. Not unusual&nbsp;
for tech startups, but this was different.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:23.360
They reported an operating loss of 420 million&nbsp;
for 2010. And two, they were running out of money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:30.640
and quick. By spring 2011, just months later,&nbsp;
