00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:04.560
When the SEC looked into Groupon, their&nbsp;
finances were a bit weird. Groupon had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:09.200
reporting revenue in a very specific way that&nbsp;
made the numbers seem bigger than they were.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:15.880
The full value of every deal sold before&nbsp;
merchants took their cut was reported as revenue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:23.080
$1.52 billion in just 6 months. Huge.&nbsp;
But under standard accounting rules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:29.320
that number wasn't real because Groupon didn't&nbsp;
keep all that money. When adjusted properly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:36.560
the real figure dropped to just 688 million, less&nbsp;
than half. That has pretty huge signs of fraud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:43.240
especially since investors had just bought shares&nbsp;
thinking Groupon was making serious bank. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:52.040
it got much worse. The $688 million figure itself&nbsp;
was still under investigation. Why? Well, instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:59.120
of using standard accounting methods, Groupon&nbsp;
introduced its own metric called ACS SOI. Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:04.160
they invented their own accounting method to&nbsp;
look profitable. Not only that, they weren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:10.320
treating marketing as a normal expense. Instead,&nbsp;
they classified it as a long-term investment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:16.440
This meant losses appeared smaller while profits&nbsp;
looked larger. And this wasn't the only issue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:20.320
Another flaw in the reporting system&nbsp;
had been there from the very beginning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:27.200
The pitch sounded simple. Offer a 50% discount,&nbsp;
then give Groupon half of what you make. A $100&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:35.360
service becomes just $25 for the business, and in&nbsp;
return, new customers, but not loyal customers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:40.640
A study from Boston University analyzed&nbsp;
over 16,692 deals [music] and found that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:46.080
customers who used Groupon gave it strikingly&nbsp;
lower rating scores. On average, they were 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:53.640
or even 20% lower. And these customers weren't&nbsp;
loyal to Groupon. They were loyal to the discount.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:58.440
Research from Harvard Business School found&nbsp;
that many businesses were actually losing money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:06.400
So merchants just stopped signing up. Just&nbsp;
months after its IPO, it was all falling apart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

